" [:option {:value "925600"} "1904"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/766"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "American Football, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} " 8 August 1932"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic (non-medal)"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/300"} "Los Angeles Memorial Coliseum, Los Angeles, California"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/900057"} "40 from 1 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "American football was demonstrated within the " [:a {:href "/venues/300"} "Los Angeles Memorial Coliseum"] " on 8 August, at 8 in the evening, the only time it has been a demonstration sport at the Olympics. In " [:a {:href "/results/925600"} "1904"] ", a series of college games in the St. Louis area is described in the " [:strong {} "1904 Official Report (Spalding Version)"] ", but they had no significant connection to the Olympic Games."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The 1932 demonstration was a single game played by American college players from the East, representing Yale, Harvard, and Princeton; and the West, representing Cal Berkeley, Stanford, and Southern Cal. Newspapers noted that the game was a test of football’s new rules. The game was scoreless for three quarters, but the East team led until deep in the fourth quarter, 6-0, after a touchdown by " [:a {:href "/athletes/902281"} "Burton Strange"] ", but " [:a {:href "/athletes/902271"} "Eddie Mays, Jr."] " missed the extra point attempt. When " [:a {:href "/athletes/902279"} "Gus Shaver"] ", of Southern Cal, scored for the West with seconds remaining on a fourth-down play, the extra point by " [:a {:href "/athletes/902268"} "Ed Kirwan"] " gave the West the victory, 7-6."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The head coaches for the two teams were " [:a {:href "/athletes/2306701"} "Howard Jones"] ", coach of the University of Southern California at the time, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/2306702"} "Tad Jones"] ", former head coach at Yale. They were brothers. The assistant coach for the West team, under Howard Jones, was Glenn “Pop” Warner, who had coached " [:a {:href "/athletes/79125"} "Jim Thorpe"] " at Carlisle Indian School."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} "West"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:class "bib"} "44"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/902250"} "Garrett Arbelbide"] " "] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:class "bib"} "7"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/902258"} "Chuck Ehrhorn"] " "] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:class "bib"} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/902267"} "Ray Hulen"] " "] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:class "bib"} "47"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/902286"} "Stanley Williamson"] " "] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:class "bib"} "26"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/902266"} "Pete Heiser"] " "] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:class "bib"} "33"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/902262"} "Bob Hall"] " "] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:class "bib"} "20"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/902280"} "Ralph Stone"] " "] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:class "bib"}